<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <AssemblyName>DiagnosticCatalog.Self</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DiagnosticCatalog.Self</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    This catalogue rides the 'lib' train, and it is the only one that does — the other three ride
    their own so they can follow an outside vendor's releases without dragging anything else along.

    Here the vendor is us. The catalogue is generated from DiagnosticCatalog.Analyzers, which ships on
    'lib', and the two must never describe different rule sets: a catalogue announcing a DCAT id the
    shipped analyzer does not report, or missing one it does, is exactly the silent mismatch this
    library exists to prevent. Versioning them together is what makes that impossible rather than
    merely unlikely.

    ADR-0015 — a catalogue's version runs on its own line — is about a catalogue whose pace is set by
    an external vendor. That reasoning does not reach this one, because the pace is this repository's.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ReleaseTrain>lib</ReleaseTrain>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Package">
    <PackageId>DiagnosticCatalog.Self</PackageId>
    <DevelopmentDependency>false</DevelopmentDependency>
    <Title>DiagnosticCatalog.Self</Title>
    <Description>The DiagnosticCatalog analyzers' own DCAT rules as strongly referenced constants, so that a project suppressing one of them takes a compile-checked reference instead of a magic string. Generated from those analyzers' DiagnosticDescriptor instances by this repository's own generator.</Description>
    <PackageTags>roslyn;analyzers;diagnostics;suppressmessage;code-analysis;catalog;dcat</PackageTags>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.en.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <Authors>Reefact</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) Reefact</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Reefact/diagnostic-catalog</PackageProjectUrl>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.en.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A PROJECT reference, where the other three catalogues take a PackageReference. They must, because
    they ride a different train from the foundation and packing against the project would stamp a
    dependency on a lib version that was never published (ADR-0007). This one rides 'lib' itself, so
    the two are packed and published at the same version by the same tag, and the reference is
    exactly what pack.sh's cross-train guard is written to allow.

    NOT PrivateAssets="all": the foundation must reach whoever consumes this catalogue, so that
    [DiagnosticRule] resolves for them (§16.1).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DiagnosticCatalog\DiagnosticCatalog.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
